From b6e03ffb307cf24b52b2488a3977e3e37e290370 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: LegendaryFire <tristan.balon@outlook.com>
Date: Fri, 20 Nov 2026 23:02:05 -0700
Subject: [PATCH] Draft Inkling 2's tokens from the conversation and verify
 them

A decode step wrote one token, so a long context paid its whole cache
read (from RAM when tiered) for each one. Now a step drafts the tokens
that followed the latest earlier occurrence of the last 4, 3 or 2
tokens (prompt lookup, no draft model) and runs them with the pending
token in one forward of up to 8 rows. Each row's token is picked as
plain decoding would pick it: a draft stands when it is that token
(greedy), or with its probability under sampling, a turned-down draft
left out of the redraw, so the output distribution is unchanged. The
short-conv histories wait for the verdict (a verify forward keeps its
conv inputs and commits the rows kept); the caches are written by
position, so the position moving back is all a rejection needs.

The draft length comes from the measured acceptance and the measured
time of a forward of each row count (an extra row costs about 7-10 ms on
2x RTX 3060, mostly its experts), so a step drafts nothing when that
does not pay. --lookup-max-t N caps a step's drafts (the main engine's
name for its lookup drafting; 0 turns it off, default 7: --draft is the
server's drafter switch and does not reach the engine).

Greedy output is identical with and without (checked at 1.5K, 24K and
58K). Copying from the context: 1.5K 40.6 -> 54.7 tok/s, 24K 29.9 ->
35.7, 58K tiered (8K window) 19.4 -> 42.0 (3.4 tokens a forward, 92%
of drafted tokens kept). Prose that copies little: 30.0 -> 29.0 at 24K.

Through the server (2 lanes, 65K context, the model card's sampling,
~15K-token prompts): an edit that returns a function 42.3 -> 66.3
tok/s, its follow-up 42.4 -> 69.8, prose 42.4 -> 42.8.
